<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch6-page3-folk-theorem" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Folk Theorem</title>

  <p>
    On the last page, we found that cooperating forever is a Nash
    equilibrium of the repeated Wine Merchant game when
    <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{4}</m>. Are there other Nash equilibria? The
    Folk Theorem says there are a <em>lot</em> of them. (A <q>folk
    theorem</q> is a result that everyone seems to know, but nobody knows
    who proved it first.)
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 6.8.1 Payoff vectors                                         -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-6-8-1">
    <title>Section 6.8.1, Payoff Vectors</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 6.8 up through Subsection 6.8.1.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      This subsection describes all the payoff pairs that the two players
      can get in a single round of the game, if they use mixed strategies.
      The four-step construction is easier to see than to read, so use the
      interactive below alongside it.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    In the interactive, the Wine Merchant game uses the book's labels:
    <m>(a, b) = (1, 1)</m>, <m>(c, d) = (-4, 4)</m>,
    <m>(e, f) = (4, -4)</m>, and <m>(g, h) = (0, 0)</m>. Here <m>p</m> is
    the probability that the Merchant sells good wine, and <m>q</m> is the
    probability that Bernard pays. The slider <m>q</m> places
    <m>r(q)</m> on one edge and <m>s(q)</m> on another (steps 1 and 2 in
    the book), and <m>p</m> slides the point along the dashed segment
    between them (step 3). The shaded triangle is the set of all possible
    payoff vectors. The darker part is where both payoffs are positive,
    just like Figure 6.8.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-payoff-region">
    <caption>Possible payoff vectors for the Wine Merchant and the Connoisseur.</caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-payoff-region"
      iframe="ch6-graph/payoff-region.html" width="100%" aspect="1:1">
      <static>
        <p>
          The triangle with corners <m>(-4, 4)</m>, <m>(1, 1)</m>, and
          <m>(4, -4)</m>, with sliders for <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> showing the
          points <m>r(q)</m>, <m>s(q)</m>, and
          <m>p \cdot r(q) + (1-p) \cdot s(q)</m>.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <exercise xml:id="check-payoff-half-half">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Both players flip a fair coin each round to decide whether to
        cooperate, so <m>p = q = \frac{1}{2}</m>. What is the payoff vector?
        Check your answer with the interactive.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>\left(\frac{1}{4}, \frac{1}{4}\right)</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Each of the four outcomes happens with probability
            <m>\frac{1}{4}</m>, so the payoff vector is
            <m>\frac{1}{4}\big((1,1) + (-4,4) + (4,-4) + (0,0)\big) = \left(\frac{1}{4}, \frac{1}{4}\right)</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>\left(\frac{1}{2}, \frac{1}{2}\right)</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. That's halfway between <m>(1,1)</m> and
            <m>(0,0)</m>, but all four outcomes are possible here, each with
            probability <m>\frac{1}{4}</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>(0, 0)</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. The <m>-4</m> and <m>4</m> cancel out, but the
            <m>(1,1)</m> outcome is still there.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 6.8.2 Minimax payoffs                                        -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-6-8-2">
    <title>Section 6.8.2, Minimax Payoffs</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 6.8.2.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      This is short, but read it slowly. There's a trap here, explained
      below.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    <alert>Warning: minimax is not maximin!</alert> In Section 5.5 we met
    the <em>maximin</em> payoff. The <em>minimax</em> payoff in this section
    sounds almost the same, but it's a different idea.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>Maximin</alert> (Section 5.5): <em>you</em> choose first,
        expecting the worst. For each of your strategies, find its worst
        case, then pick the best of those. This is the most you can
        guarantee yourself.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>Minimax</alert> (Section 6.8.2): your <em>opponent</em>
        chooses first, trying to hurt you, and you best-respond. For each of
        the opponent's strategies, find your best response, then take the
        worst of those. This is the harshest punishment the opponent can
        force on you.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The two can be different. In Matching Pennies, if we only allow pure
    strategies, your maximin payoff is <m>-1</m> (any coin you choose can be
    beaten), but your minimax payoff is <m>+1</m> (once you see your
    opponent's coin, you can always match it).
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-minimax-formula">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Build the formula for Player 1's minimax payoff <m>m_1</m> by putting
        the blocks in order. Here <m>s_i</m> are Player 1's pure strategies
        and <m>t_j</m> are Player 2's. Not every block belongs!
      </p>
    </statement>
    <blocks layout="horizontal">
      <block><m>m_1 =</m></block>
      <block><m>\min_{t_j}</m></block>
      <block><m>\max_{s_i}</m></block>
      <block><m>\pi_1(s_i, t_j)</m></block>
      <block correct="no"><m>\max_{t_j}</m></block>
      <block correct="no"><m>\min_{s_i}</m></block>
      <block correct="no"><m>\pi_2(s_i, t_j)</m></block>
    </blocks>
    <hint>
      <p>
        The operation on the inside happens first. Player 1's best response
        comes first, and then Player 2 picks the strategy that makes that
        best response as bad as possible.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle m_1 = \min_{t_j} \max_{s_i} \pi_1(s_i, t_j)</m>.
        Compare this to the maximin payoff from Section 5.5, where the max
        is on the outside and the min is on the inside.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-minimax-chicken">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Recall Chicken from Section 3.2.3:
      </p>
      <table xml:id="table-chicken-minimax">
      <title>Chicken</title>
      <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
        <col right="minor"/>
        <col/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell></cell>
          <cell>Teenager 2: straight</cell>
          <cell>Teenager 2: swerve</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Teenager 1: straight</cell>
          <cell>(<m>-2</m>, <m>-2</m>)</cell>
          <cell>(1, <m>-1</m>)</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Teenager 1: swerve</cell>
          <cell>(<m>-1</m>, 1)</cell>
          <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
      </table>
      <p>What is Teenager 1's minimax payoff <m>m_1</m>?</p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>-1</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. If Teenager 2 goes straight, Teenager 1's best response
            is to swerve, for <m>-1</m>. If Teenager 2 swerves, Teenager 1's
            best response is to go straight, for 1. The worst of these is
            <m>-1</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>-2</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. <m>-2</m> is the worst payoff in Teenager 1's column,
            but minimax assumes Teenager 1 best-responds. If Teenager 2 goes
            straight, Teenager 1 would swerve rather than crash.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>1</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. 1 is Teenager 1's best response payoff when Teenager
            2 swerves. But Teenager 2 gets to pick the strategy that hurts
            Teenager 1 the most, so take the <em>minimum</em> over Teenager
            2's choices.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 6.8.3-6.8.4 The Folk Theorem                                 -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-6-8-3">
    <title>Sections 6.8.3 and 6.8.4, The Folk Theorem</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Sections 6.8.3 and 6.8.4.</alert> Read the statement of
      Theorem 6.2 and the proof of part (1) carefully.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>You can skip the proof of part (2)</alert>, which starts with
      <q>We will not show (2) in general.</q> The idea is all you need: the
      players take turns among the four outcomes in just the right
      proportions, so the <em>average</em> payoff is <m>(v_1, v_2)</m>. If
      anyone ever deviates, the other player punishes them forever, holding
      them down to their minimax payoff.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    In plain words, the Folk Theorem says: any payoff vector where both
    players get more than their minimax payoffs can be the average payoff of
    a Nash equilibrium, as long as the players care enough about the
    future. For the Wine Merchant, that's the whole dark region of the
    triangle.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="reflect-folk-part-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Read the statement of part (1) again, and then the proof. Does the
        proof actually show what the statement says? Look closely at the
        words <q>in every round.</q>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        No! The proof shows that Player 1 has a strategy that guarantees
        her at least <m>m_1</m> in every round: just best-respond to
        whatever Player 2's strategy says to do next. So in total, she can
        guarantee herself at least <m>\frac{m_1}{1-\delta}</m>, and a Nash
        equilibrium has to give her at least that much. But that's a
        statement about her <em>total</em> payoff, not her payoff in each
        round.
      </p>
      <p>
        In fact, the statement is false as written. In the Wine Merchant
        game, both minimax payoffs are 0. Consider these strategies: in
        round 0, the Merchant sells good wine and Bernard steals it. After
        that, both cooperate forever. If anyone ever deviates from this
        plan, both defect forever. You can check that this is a Nash
        equilibrium when <m>\delta \geq \frac{4}{5}</m>, but the Merchant
        gets <m>-4 \lt 0</m> in round 0.
      </p>
      <p>
        The correct statement is that at a Nash equilibrium, Player 1's
        <em>total</em> payoff is at least <m>\frac{m_1}{1-\delta}</m>, or
        equivalently, her <em>average</em> payoff is at least <m>m_1</m>.
        Section 6.8.4 makes the same slip: the Nash equilibria it describes
        give payoffs of <m>(v_1, v_2)</m> on average, not in every round.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Example: the cheating merchant                               -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="example-cheating-merchant">
    <title>The Cheating Merchant</title>
    <p>
      Here's an equilibrium that the Folk Theorem promises, and that might
      surprise you. Suppose the players use the following strategies:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          The Merchant sells good wine five times, then sells bad wine once,
          and repeats this forever.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Bernard pays every single time, even when he gets bad wine.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>If either player ever deviates from this plan, both defect forever.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Over each cycle of six rounds, the Merchant gets
      <m>1+1+1+1+1+4 = 9</m> and Bernard gets <m>1+1+1+1+1-4 = 1</m>. So
      the average payoffs are <m>\left(\frac{3}{2}, \frac{1}{6}\right)</m>.
      Both are above the minimax payoff 0, so by the Folk Theorem this can
      be a Nash equilibrium for large enough <m>\delta</m>. Checking every
      possible deviation, it is a Nash equilibrium when <m>\delta</m> is at
      least about <m>0.97</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Bernard knowingly accepts being cheated once every six rounds. Why?
      Because if he ever steals, the relationship ends, and he gets 0
      forever after. Getting cheated occasionally still beats that, on
      average.
    </p>
  </example>

  <exercise xml:id="check-cheating-merchant-delta">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Plain cooperation needs <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{4}</m>, but the
        cheating merchant equilibrium needs <m>\delta</m> close to
        <m>0.97</m>. In which round is Bernard most tempted to steal?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement><p>The round when he gets bad wine.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Close, but not quite. Stealing bad wine gets him 0 instead of
            <m>-4</m>, but the round before is worse.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>The round just before he gets bad wine.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Stealing then gets him 4 right away, and the very next
            thing cooperation has to offer him is the <m>-4</m> round. So
            the future has to matter a lot to keep him honest.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>The round just after he gets bad wine.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Right after being cheated, Bernard has five
            cooperative rounds ahead of him before the next <m>-4</m>, so
            cooperating looks relatively good.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Try to find the cheating merchant's payoff vector,
    <m>\left(\frac{3}{2}, \frac{1}{6}\right)</m>, on the triangle in the
    interactive above. It's on the edge between <m>(1,1)</m> and
    <m>(4,-4)</m>, which makes sense, since the only outcomes that happen
    are <m>(c, c)</m> and <m>(d, c)</m>.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="reflect-folk-theorem-news">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The Folk Theorem says that almost any outcome can be a Nash
        equilibrium of a repeated game, including outcomes where one player
        keeps getting cheated. Is this good news or bad news for game
        theory? Think about what it means for using game theory to
        <em>predict</em> what people will do.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-6-3" iframe="question-form.html?section=6.3" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
